    <h2 id="scottish-tax-bands">1. Scottish 6-Band System vs Rest of UK 3-Band System</h2>
    <p>
      The foundational difference between Scotland and the rest of the UK (rUK) lies in the structure of the tax brackets. While England, Wales, and Northern Ireland utilize a relatively streamlined 3-band structure (Basic 20%, Higher 40%, and Additional 45%), the Scottish Government has opted for a highly progressive 6-band system. This model was introduced with the stated intention of making the tax system fairer, ensuring that the lowest earners pay slightly less tax while those with higher incomes contribute significantly more to fund public services in Scotland.
    </p>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 my-6">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-blue-200 bg-blue-50/50 p-5 dark:border-blue-900 dark:bg-blue-950/30">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-blue-900 dark:text-blue-200 mt-0 mb-3">Scottish Income Tax Bands (2025/26)</h3>
        <ul class="space-y-1.5 text-sm text-blue-900 dark:text-blue-200 mb-0 pl-4 list-disc">
          <li><strong>Personal Allowance (0%):</strong> Up to £12,570. This UK-wide allowance remains unchanged and provides a tax-free foundation for all residents.</li>
          <li><strong>Starter Rate (19%):</strong> £12,571 to £14,876. A specialized lower rate designed to reduce the tax burden on part-time workers and entry-level roles.</li>
          <li><strong>Basic Rate (20%):</strong> £14,877 to £26,561. Aligned with the rUK Basic Rate, but spanning a much narrower income band.</li>
          <li><strong>Intermediate Rate (21%):</strong> £26,562 to £43,662. The point at which middle-income Scottish taxpayers begin paying more than their English counterparts.</li>
          <li><strong>Higher Rate (42%):</strong> £43,663 to £75,000. Noticeably higher than the 40% rate in rUK, and kicking in over £6,000 earlier.</li>
          <li><strong>Advanced Rate (45%):</strong> £75,001 to £125,140. Introduced as a new band in recent budgets to target upper-middle earners.</li>
          <li><strong>Top Rate (48%):</strong> Above £125,140. The highest income tax bracket in the UK, applying a 48% marginal rate to top earners.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="rounded-xl border border-emerald-200 bg-emerald-50/50 p-5 dark:border-emerald-900 dark:bg-emerald-950/30">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 mt-0 mb-3">Rest of UK Tax Bands (England, Wales, NI)</h3>
        <ul class="space-y-1.5 text-sm text-emerald-900 dark:text-emerald-200 mb-0 pl-4 list-disc">
          <li><strong>Personal Allowance (0%):</strong> Up to £12,570. Exactly the same as in Scotland.</li>
          <li><strong>Basic Rate (20%):</strong> £12,571 to £50,270. A vast band that covers the majority of the working population, keeping their marginal rate steady at 20%.</li>
          <li><strong>Higher Rate (40%):</strong> £50,271 to £125,140. The rate applied to higher earners, starting at a much higher threshold than Scotland's 42% rate.</li>
          <li><strong>Additional Rate (45%):</strong> Above £125,140. The top rate for individuals earning substantial incomes, effectively 3% lower than the Scottish Top Rate.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    
    <p>
      The psychological impact of these differing bands cannot be overstated. For an employee in England, receiving a pay rise from £40,000 to £45,000 means staying entirely within the 20% bracket. For a Scottish taxpayer, that same pay rise pushes a portion of their income into the 42% Higher Rate bracket, creating a feeling of diminishing returns on career progression. 
    </p>

    <h2 id="salary-comparison-scotland-england">2. Salary Comparison Table: Take-Home Pay Differences</h2>
    <p>
      To truly understand the impact of the devolved tax system, we must look at concrete numbers. The Scottish system is designed so that lower earners actually pay slightly less tax than they would in England, thanks to the 19% Starter Rate. However, the "crossover point" occurs at approximately £28,850. Anyone earning above this threshold in Scotland will pay more Income Tax than someone earning the exact same salary in England, Wales, or Northern Ireland. For high earners, this divergence becomes a chasm.
    </p>

